  "explanation": {
    "id": "4e7c8e75-237f-44fa-a3a9-c25be5a7a640",
    "body": "Note: in the explanation I accidentally wrote $x(t)$ instead of $y(x)$ throughout the computation for the particular solution. Treat each $x(t)$ or $x_p(t)$ term as $y(x)$ and $y_p(x)$.\n\nThe particular solution $y_p(x)$ for this inhomogeneous differential equation can be found using the variation of parameters by using the formula \\[y_p(x)=-y_1(x)\\int\\frac{y_2(x)f(x)}{det(W)}dx+y_2(x)\\int\\frac{y_1(x)f(x)}{det(W)}dx\\] Where $f(x)$ is equal to the inhomogenous portion of the differential equation (in this case 2) and $det(W)$ is the determinant of the Wronskian matrix of the given solutions to the homogeneous differential equation (in this case $x^3$). \\[\\]Plugging the given values in the formula for variation of parameters results in a particular solution of \\[y_p(x)=x^2(ln(x))^2\\]",
